{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>Are there no graves in Egypt.</b> Ramban, of blessed memory, wrote: how is it possible that those who cry out to HaShem and lift their hands and eyes to Him would turn and say to Him that it would have been better had He not saved them, and say that it is better for us to serve Egypt than to die in the wilderness? Therefore the words of our Rabbis, of blessed memory, are accepted, that they were factions: one faction cried out to HaShem — these are 'the children of Israel,' that is, the important ones among them — and this is what is written, ״וישאו בני ישראל את עיניהם״ and ״ויצעקו בני ישראל אל ה'״; and another faction denied His prophet and did not choose salvation, and of them it is written (Psalms 106:7): ״וימרו על ים בים סוף״." "<b>And they said</b> in the Midrash: four factions were at the sea. The first faction did not desire salvation, and of them Moses said, ״התיצבו וראו את ישועת ה'״, and of them the verse says, ״וימרו על ים בים סוף״. The second faction said to return to Egypt — of them the verse says, ״כי אשר ראיתם את מצרים היום וגו״. The third faction said to do battle with them — of them HaShem said, ״ה' ילחם לכם״. The fourth faction cried out to the Holy One, blessed be He, as it says, ״ויצעקו בני ישראל אל ה'״; of them He said, ״ואתם תחרישון״ — end of quotation. And Onkelos translated ״ויצעקו״ as ״וזעיקו״, rendering it in the language of murmuring, as in (II Samuel 13:19) ״ותלך הלוך וזעקה״, and (Judges 4:10) ״ויזעק ברק את זבולן ואת נפתלי״; and we find explicitly ״ואת צעקתם שמעת על ים סוף״. And so our Rabbis, of blessed memory, understood it in the sense of prayer, from what I wrote above: ״ויצעקו״ — they took up the craft of their forefathers."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:58:59.928502474Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}